@title Claude Chan
# Claude Chan (Claude娘, orange hair and chrysanthemum motif) as a static 3D figure.
# Built from her design sheet: front/side/back views, outfit pieces and accessories.
# Every part is a parametric surface (X,Y,Z,u,u0,u1,v,v0,v1). z is up, she faces -y, and +x is her left.
# Generated by generate.py in this folder: helper functions first, then the head, hair, outfit and props.

# Helpers. fw places a local shape (p,q,r) in a frame: origin o, axes (i,j,k) given per coordinate.
fw(p,q,r,o,i,j,k)=o+p*i+q*j+r*k
# Capsule profile: u runs 0..pi from one end to the other, radius r0 -> r1, rounded ends.
cf(u)=(1-cos(u))/2
cr(u,r0,r1)=(r0+(r1-r0)*cf(u))*sqrt(sqrt(1-cos(u)^4))

//Head
# The head is sculpted from parts: a face mask, the cranium under the hair, the underside of the jaw,
# the neck and the ears. The face mask is a stack of horizontal arcs, one per height h. Each arc runs from the
# side of the head around the front to the other side: half-width fwd(h), depth fd(h), and a superellipse
# exponent fq(h) that makes it boxy across the eyes (a flat front that turns quickly at the cheekbones) and
# rounder along the jaw. fpl(h) is the profile of the face seen from the side (forehead, the dip between the
# eyes, the plane of the cheeks and mouth, chin), and fws(h) the outline seen from the front: a blunt chin,
# straight jaw lines up to the cheekbones, level sides. fr adds what is narrower than the whole face: the nose, lips, chin, the
# round of each cheek, brow and eye sockets. fy(p,h) is the y of the skin at x=p, z=h.
smn(a,b,c)=(a+b-sqrt((a-b)^2+c^2))/2
fpl(h)=(-0.44)+(-1.96757)*(h-10.96)+358.39167*max(0,h-10.96)^3+(-515.15703)*max(0,h-10.981)^3+165.73619*max(0,h-11.029)^3+(-22.44302)*max(0,h-11.097)^3+25.97357*max(0,h-11.185)^3+(-15.0632)*max(0,h-11.26)^3+6.05913*max(0,h-11.35)^3+(-8.85454)*max(0,h-11.465)^3+4.83821*max(0,h-11.58)^3+(-5.88354)*max(0,h-11.68)^3+15.03499*max(0,h-11.76)^3+(-8.60339)*max(0,h-11.85)^3+0.26932*max(0,h-11.95)^3+(-0.18229)*max(0,h-12.11)^3+0.22663*max(0,h-12.25)^3+(-1.885)*max(0,h-12.39)^3
fws(h)=0.06+3.55346*(h-10.96)+(-429.77289)*max(0,h-10.96)^3+822.96473*max(0,h-10.99)^3+(-477.67364)*max(0,h-11.03)^3+102.7524*max(0,h-11.08)^3+(-22.87119)*max(0,h-11.13)^3+12.73235*max(0,h-11.18)^3+(-4.05822)*max(0,h-11.23)^3+(-20.49949)*max(0,h-11.28)^3+62.05617*max(0,h-11.33)^3+(-90.86228)*max(0,h-11.38)^3+59.3934*max(0,h-11.44)^3+(-19.99534)*max(0,h-11.54)^3+12.09154*max(0,h-11.64)^3+(-6.58044)*max(0,h-11.73)^3+(-0.16626)*max(0,h-11.92)^3+0.28055*max(0,h-12.22)^3+3.38314*max(0,h-12.51)^3
fwd(h)=fws(h)*sqrt(max(0,h-10.96)/(h-10.925))
fd(h)=smn(0.1-fpl(h),1.18*fwd(h),0.08)
fq(h)=0.3-0.11*exp(-((h-11.73)/0.14)^2)+0.07/(1+exp((h-11.43)/0.07))
fno(h)=0.036*exp(-(max(0,h-11.475)/0.07)^1.5-(max(0,11.475-h)/0.035)^2)
fr(p,h)=-fno(h)*exp(-(p/(0.034+0.024*exp(-((h-11.465)/0.05)^2)))^2)-0.008*exp(-((h-11.46)/0.06)^2-(p/0.1)^2)-0.008*exp(-((h-11.305)/0.03)^2-(p/0.085)^2)+0.006*exp(-((h-11.27)/0.012)^2-(p/0.08)^2)-0.008*exp(-((h-11.23)/0.028)^2-(p/0.065)^2)-0.006*exp(-((h-11.07)/0.065)^2-(p/0.1)^2)+0.02*exp(-((abs(p)-0.33)/0.17)^2-((h-11.75)/0.1)^2)-0.008*exp(-((abs(p)-0.3)/0.2)^2-((h-11.97)/0.07)^2)-0.04*exp(-((abs(p)-0.3)/0.2)^2-((h-11.51)/0.17)^2)-0.02*exp(-((abs(p)-0.26)/0.18)^2-((h-11.29)/0.13)^2)
fy(p,h)=fpl(h)+fd(h)*(1-max(0,1-(p/fwd(h))^2)^fq(h))+fr(p,h)
# The mask is drawn as nine patches of the same surface, three across and three up: cheek, nose and
# mouth, cheek; eye, bridge of the nose, eye; and the brow. So the cheeks, nose, lips and chin each get
# their own fine mesh. Patches share their edges exactly, so no seam shows.
(0.97*(fwd(h)*sin(f))+0.06976*(fpl(h)+fd(h)*(1-abs(cos(f))^(2*fq(h)))+fr(fwd(h)*sin(f),h))+(-0.23288)*(h)+2.62314,(-0.06783)*(fwd(h)*sin(f))+0.99756*(fpl(h)+fd(h)*(1-abs(cos(f))^(2*fq(h)))+fr(fwd(h)*sin(f),h))+0.01628*(h)+(-0.18372),0.23345*(fwd(h)*sin(f))+0.97237*(h)+0.31222,f,-pi/2,(-0.36),h,10.96,11.66) | ref=cheekRight color=#e5d0cd
